/security-review — R7 round 2 — range 55be3bed..75463772 (fix delta 68c95758..75463772 examined)

NO FINDINGS — Verified with Python `re` against the shipped pre-commit-config.yaml (`(?x)` verbose, no wrapping group): `lib/foo.turbo.ts` → no match; `.turbo/x`, `packages/a/.turbo/x` → match; `(^|/)[^/]*-lock\.json$` matches `a/b-lock.json` and `foo-lock.json` only, refuses `a-lock.json/c`, `x-lock.jsonx`, `lib/lock.json`; `rebuild/x`, `src/builder/x`, `nodes/x` → no match. Every construct used (`(^|/)`, `\.`, `[^/]*`, `$`, no lookaround/backrefs) is valid Go RE2, so the gitleaks `[allowlist] paths` entries compile and behave identically under `regexp.MatchString`.

Widening check: gitleaks went strictly narrower — old `build/*` was "build" plus zero-or-more slashes, i.e. any path containing the substring (`rebuild/`, `lib/build.ts`); new requires a full segment. The pre-commit global exclude moved from root-anchored to any-depth for the directory entries, but that only aligns it with the `**/dir/**` globs dprint/taplo already carried, and the `sec` hook is `always_run` + `pass_filenames: false` so the secret scan is unaffected by the exclude. Lockfile alternatives are equivalent to the old `.*-lock\.json$` in reach.

Checker (scripts/src/check.ts): the `(^|/)` normaliser is anchored to the literal token, the new `tomlStringList` body regex only widens what a `]` inside a quoted literal parses to (not an allowlist), the parse-failure path now reports rather than skips, and rule 11's absolute/`..` refusal is correct for `/`-separated globs. Inputs are repo-authored pack files, not attacker-controlled. Nothing reaches the >80% concrete-attack-path bar.
